# .installignore — source under src/ that does not ship.
#
# TWO readers, and an edit here moves both.
#
# 1. The Python installer (packages/installer), at the staging step. This is the
#    pruning role the file was written for: a pattern here keeps matching source
#    out of the deployed fileset.
# 2. `make content-lint`, as the register of *deliberately* unstaged source. That
#    gate reports a directory staging never reads, because such a directory is
#    normally content nobody wired up. A directory pattern here says the opposite
#    — this one is source-side on purpose — so the gate stays quiet about it.
#
# The consequence worth knowing before you add a line: a directory pattern can
# only ever SILENCE content-lint, and it does so wherever the gate looks, not
# only where staging would have pruned. Adding one for a deploy reason therefore
# narrows what the gate measures, so say which of the two roles you mean.
#
# That is also the one bound on what a directory pattern may say: it has to name
# something. A directory pattern with no alphanumeric character in it — '*/',
# '.*/' — excludes by shape rather than by name, taking directories nobody has
# written yet, and content-lint fails the run on it. A glob that keeps a name in
# it ('*cache*/') is fine.
#
# scripts/install.sh is a thin exec stub that delegates to the Python
# installer and never reads this file itself — there is no bash grammar to
# keep in parity with.
#
# Grammar: one pattern per line; '#' comment lines and blank lines are
# ignored.
#
# - A trailing '/' marks a DIRECTORY pattern (matched against directory names
#   only); without it, the pattern is a FILE pattern (matched against file
#   basenames only). Excluding a directory prunes its whole subtree — nothing
#   beneath it needs its own entry.
# - A leading '/' ANCHORS the pattern to the direct children of a staged
#   namespace subdirectory (skills/, rules/, agents/, commands/, hooks/).
#   Without a leading '/', the pattern matches at ANY depth, including inside
#   a DIR item's own interior (e.g. a skill's nested scripts/ subdir) — depth
#   an anchored pattern can never reach, since a skill directory stages as one
#   DIR item and its contents are never walked against an anchored entry.
# - '*', '?' and '[...]' make the pattern a glob, matched with
#   fnmatch.fnmatchcase against a single path component (case-sensitive). A
#   name with none of those characters matches exactly.
# - A '/' anywhere other than the leading or trailing position is a parse
#   error. No '**', no negation.
#
# Anchoring is explicit, not inferred from glob-ness, so each entry states the
# depth it means rather than having depth read off whether it happens to
# contain a '*'.
#
# The real tool-root instruction files are *.md.template and are NOT matched by
# these bare basenames.
#
# AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md / GEMINI.md are UNANCHORED: in this repo those names are
# the convention for guidance addressed to agents working on the source, at
# whatever depth they sit. A skill that grows its own AGENTS.md explaining its
# scripts to a maintainer is repo-side documentation, and anchoring these would
# ship it to every install carrying this repo's vocabulary — the same leak the
# dev-artifact patterns below exist to stop. Live leakers today: AGENTS.md as a
# direct child of rules/ and of skills/, in both the shared and the Claude
# trees. CLAUDE.md and GEMINI.md match nothing yet and are class coverage.
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md
GEMINI.md
# README.md stays ANCHORED, because unlike the three above it is genuinely
# ambiguous: a skill's own references/README.md could be content it means to
# ship. Nothing legitimate matches it at depth today, so this is the cautious
# side of a real judgement call rather than a live exclusion.
/README.md
# Source-only rationale docs, per the plugin layout table in src/plugins/AGENTS.md.
# Two locations, and this one entry now declares both. At a namespace child it
# prunes: staging would otherwise ship it. At a tool root it declares: staging
# never read there, so nothing needed pruning — but content-lint now reports an
# unread directory as a defect, and without a declaration it would fail the build
# for whoever follows that layout table.
/rules-readmes/
# Dev-only artifacts that must never deploy, at ANY depth — including inside a
# DIR item's own interior (a skill's scripts/ subdir, most visibly). The
# Python and shell patterns mirror the repo gate's suite discovery (*_test.<ext>
# or test_*.<ext>) deliberately: a file the gate runs as a repo-side suite must
# not be a file the installer ships. Widening one without the other lets a
# suite be both executed here and deployed downstream. Confirmed live leakers:
# ruff-postedit_test.sh and codex-broker-reaper_test.sh, both direct children
# of the hooks/ namespace dir — caught by class (below) rather than by name,
# so a future hook's own *_test.sh suite is covered without a new entry.
*_test.py
test_*.py
*_test.js
*_test.sh
test_*.sh
*.pyc
__pycache__/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
